    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d739">

                <pb facs="0707" n="703" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg703"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/624: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-05-06T16:00:00-04:00">May 6, 1938—4 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>144. Your 227, May 2, 4 p.m., Chinese Maritime Customs. On May 3 the Chinese
                    Embassy at Washington left with the Department a memorandum<anchor n="87"
                        xml:id="d739-f1"/> summarizing the arrangement arrived at between the
                    British Ambassador to Japan and the Japanese Foreign Office, and on May 5 a
                        memorandum<anchor n="87" xml:id="d739-f2"/> containing the text of a <hi
                        rendition="#italic">note verb die</hi> communicated to the British Foreign
                    Office by the Chinese Embassy at London and containing an account of personal
                    observations made by the Chinese Ambassador at London to Leith-Ross.<anchor
                        n="88" xml:id="d739-f3"/> The Department assumes that the substance of these
                    communications is known to you. Copies of the two memoranda are being forwarded
                    by pouch.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
